## 1. How HTTP Works

HTTP is a stateless, application-layer protocol that follows a request-response cycle. The client (browser, mobile app, another service) opens a connection, sends a request, and waits for the server's response. **Stateless** means the server has no memory of previous requests — every request is independent. This is why mechanisms like cookies and tokens exist: to bolt state on top of a stateless protocol.

HTTP runs over TCP (or QUIC in HTTP/3). For HTTPS, a TLS handshake happens before any HTTP traffic, encrypting everything on the wire.

---

## 2. HTTP Messages

Every HTTP interaction is a request + response pair. Both share the same structure: a start line, headers, a blank line, and an optional body.

### Request
The start line contains the method, the path, and the HTTP version. Headers follow, and if there's a body (POST, PUT, PATCH), it comes after the blank line.

```
POST /api/users HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
Content-Type: application/json
Content-Length: 42

{"name": "Alice", "email": "alice@example.com"}
```

### Response
The start line contains the HTTP version, status code, and a reason phrase. Headers and an optional body follow.

```
HTTP/1.1 201 Created
Content-Type: application/json
Location: /api/users/123

{"id": 123, "name": "Alice"}
```

As a backend developer, you're always constructing responses — choosing the right status code, setting the right headers, and deciding what goes in the body.

