## 3. Request Methods

Each method signals the **intent** of the request. Two properties matter a lot for how you design APIs and handle failures:

- **Safe** — the request doesn't change server state. GET, HEAD, OPTIONS are safe. This means clients and intermediaries can freely retry them.
- **Idempotent** — repeating the same request N times has the same effect as doing it once. GET, PUT, DELETE are idempotent. This is critical for retry logic: if a PUT times out, the client can safely retry it.

| Method | Safe | Idempotent | Has Body | Purpose |
|--------|------|-----------|----------|---------|
| `GET` | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | Retrieve a resource |
| `HEAD` | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | Same as GET but no response body — useful to check if a resource exists or get its metadata |
| `POST` | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ | Create a resource or submit data. Not idempotent — repeating a POST creates duplicates |
| `PUT` | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | Replace a resource entirely. Idempotent — sending the same PUT twice is safe |
| `PATCH` | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ | Partial update. Not inherently idempotent — depends on how you implement it |
| `DELETE` | ❌ | ✅ | ❌ | Remove a resource. Idempotent — deleting an already-deleted resource should return 404 or 204, not crash |
| `OPTIONS` | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | Ask the server what methods are supported. Browsers send this automatically as a CORS preflight |
| `CONNECT` | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | Used by proxies to establish a TCP tunnel (e.g. for HTTPS through a proxy) |

**PUT vs PATCH:** PUT means "replace this resource with exactly what I'm sending." PATCH means "apply these changes to the resource." For a user profile, PUT would require sending all fields; PATCH lets you send only `{"bio": "new bio"}`.

