## 4. Status Codes

Status codes tell the client what happened. As a backend developer, choosing the right code is part of your API contract — clients and intermediaries make decisions based on them.

### 1xx — Informational

**`100 Continue`** — The server is telling the client "I received your headers, go ahead and send the body." Used when a client wants to check if the server will accept a large upload before committing to sending it (via `Expect: 100-continue`).

**`101 Switching Protocols`** — The server agrees to upgrade the connection to a different protocol. This is the response that kicks off a WebSocket connection.

### 2xx — Success

**`200 OK`** — The most common success code. Used for GET responses, successful updates, anything that doesn't fit a more specific code.

**`201 Created`** — A new resource was created. Always pair this with a `Location` header pointing to the newly created resource. This is what you return after a successful POST that creates something.

**`202 Accepted`** — The request has been received and queued but not yet processed. Use this for async operations — for example, triggering a background job. The response body should explain how the client can check on progress.

**`204 No Content`** — Success, but there's nothing to return. The right code for DELETE responses or PUT/PATCH when you don't need to return the updated resource. A 204 must not include a response body.

**`206 Partial Content`** — The server is returning only part of a resource in response to a Range request. Used for video streaming and resumable downloads.

### 3xx — Redirections

**`301 Moved Permanently`** — The resource has permanently moved. Browsers and search engines cache this and update their records. Note: many clients change the method to GET on redirect, even if the original was a POST.

**`302 Found`** — Temporary redirect. Same method-changing behavior as 301. Use 307 instead if you need to preserve the method.

**`303 See Other`** — Always redirects to the target as a GET, regardless of the original method. This is the right choice for the **Post/Redirect/Get (PRG) pattern**: after a POST, redirect with 303 to a GET URL so the user doesn't resubmit on browser refresh.

**`304 Not Modified`** — The resource hasn't changed since the client last fetched it. Sent in response to conditional requests (`If-None-Match` / `If-Modified-Since`). The client should use its cached copy. No body is sent.

**`307 Temporary Redirect`** — Temporary redirect that strictly preserves the original HTTP method. If the original was POST, the redirect is also POST.

**`308 Permanent Redirect`** — Permanent redirect that strictly preserves the original method. Use for permanent URL migrations when method preservation matters.

### 4xx — Client Errors

These mean the client did something wrong. Don't return 500 for bad input — that's a 4xx.

**`400 Bad Request`** — The request is malformed or invalid in some general way. Use this when the JSON is unparseable, required fields are missing, or query params are wrong. Be specific in the response body about what exactly is wrong.

**`401 Unauthorized`** — Despite the name, this means **not authenticated**. The client hasn't proven who they are. Return a `WWW-Authenticate` header telling them how to authenticate. Clients should retry with credentials.

**`403 Forbidden`** — The client is authenticated, but doesn't have permission to access this resource. Unlike 401, re-authenticating won't help. Sometimes it's better to return 404 instead of 403 to avoid revealing that a resource even exists.

**`404 Not Found`** — The resource doesn't exist. Also appropriate when you want to hide a resource from unauthorized users — returning 403 would confirm the resource exists.

**`405 Method Not Allowed`** — The resource exists but doesn't support this method (e.g. POST to a read-only endpoint). Always include an `Allow` header listing the supported methods: `Allow: GET, OPTIONS`.

**`408 Request Timeout`** — The server timed out waiting for the client to finish sending the request. The client may retry.

**`409 Conflict`** — The request conflicts with the current state of the resource. Classic use cases: trying to create a resource that already exists, or a version conflict in optimistic locking.

**`410 Gone`** — Like 404, but signals the resource existed before and has been permanently removed. Use this when you want to explicitly tell clients (and search engines) a URL is dead forever, not just currently missing.

**`413 Content Too Large`** — The request body exceeds the server's configured limit. Return this when enforcing upload size limits.

**`415 Unsupported Media Type`** — The client sent a `Content-Type` the server doesn't handle. For example, your JSON API receiving `text/xml`.

**`422 Unprocessable Content`** — The request is well-formed and the right content type, but the content fails semantic validation. This is the right code for **validation errors** — the JSON parsed fine, but the email address is invalid or a required field failed a business rule. Prefer this over 400 for validation.

**`429 Too Many Requests`** — The client has exceeded a rate limit. Always include a `Retry-After` header telling the client when they can try again.

### 5xx — Server Errors

**`500 Internal Server Error`** — An unhandled exception or unexpected condition. This is the generic catch-all. Make sure your logs have enough context to debug these. Never expose stack traces to the client.

**`501 Not Implemented`** — The server doesn't support the functionality required for this request. Use when a method is understood but not implemented.

**`502 Bad Gateway`** — Your server is acting as a proxy and received an invalid response from the upstream service. Common when a downstream microservice is down or returning garbage.

**`503 Service Unavailable`** — The server is temporarily unable to handle requests — overloaded or in maintenance. Include a `Retry-After` header. This is the right code during graceful shutdown or when a circuit breaker trips.

**`504 Gateway Timeout`** — Your server didn't receive a timely response from an upstream service. Different from 502 — the upstream simply didn't respond in time.

