## 5. Important Headers

### Headers Your Server Must Read

**`Host`** — Required in HTTP/1.1. Tells you which virtual host the client is targeting. Critical if you're serving multiple domains from one server.

**`Content-Type`** — The media type of the request body. Always check this before parsing. If a client sends `application/xml` to your JSON endpoint, reject it with 415.

**`Authorization`** — Carries the client's credentials. The value is `<scheme> <credentials>`, typically `Bearer <jwt>` for APIs.

**`Cookie`** — Sends all cookies the client has stored for your domain. Used for session-based auth.

**`Origin`** — Set by browsers on cross-origin requests. Your CORS logic reads this to decide whether to allow the request.

**`X-Forwarded-For`** / **`Forwarded`** — When you're behind a reverse proxy, the client's real IP comes in one of these headers rather than the TCP connection source. `X-Forwarded-For` is the non-standard but widely-used version; `Forwarded` is the RFC standard. Never trust these blindly — a client can spoof them. Only trust them if the request came from a known trusted proxy IP.

**`X-Forwarded-Proto`** — Tells you whether the original client request was HTTP or HTTPS. Important when your app is behind an SSL-terminating proxy — the connection to your app is HTTP, but the original client connection was HTTPS.

**`If-None-Match`** / **`If-Modified-Since`** — Sent by clients for cache validation. Handle these and return `304` when the resource hasn't changed to save bandwidth.

### Headers Your Server Must Set

**`Content-Type`** — Always set this on responses with a body. For JSON APIs: `Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8`. If you don't set it, browsers may guess (MIME sniffing), which is a security issue.

**`Location`** — Required with `201 Created` (points to the new resource) and all `3xx` redirects (points to the target URL).

**`Cache-Control`** — Tells caches and browsers how long to store this response. Covered in depth in the Caching section.

**`ETag`** — A version identifier for the resource. Clients send it back in `If-None-Match` for cache validation. Covered in Conditional Requests.

**`Retry-After`** — Required with `429` and `503`. Value is either a number of seconds or an HTTP date. Clients and load balancers use this to back off intelligently.

**`WWW-Authenticate`** — Required with `401`. Tells the client what auth scheme to use: `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer realm="api"`.

**`Allow`** — Required with `405`. Lists the HTTP methods the endpoint supports: `Allow: GET, POST, OPTIONS`.

**`Vary`** — Tells caches which request headers affect the response. If you serve gzip and uncompressed responses, set `Vary: Accept-Encoding` so caches store them separately.

**`Server-Timing`** — Lets you expose server-side performance metrics to the client for debugging: `Server-Timing: db;dur=12, auth;dur=3`. Visible in browser DevTools.

