## 7. Cookies & Sessions

HTTP is stateless, but cookies let the server store a small piece of data on the client that gets sent back with every subsequent request to the same origin. This is the foundation of session-based authentication.

**Flow:** Server sends `Set-Cookie: session_id=abc123` → browser stores it → browser attaches `Cookie: session_id=abc123` to every subsequent request to that domain.

As a backend developer, you control the `Set-Cookie` attributes, which determine the security and lifetime of your cookies.

**`HttpOnly`** — The cookie is not accessible to JavaScript. This is a critical protection against XSS attacks stealing session tokens. Always set this for session cookies.

**`Secure`** — The cookie is only sent over HTTPS. Always set this in production.

**`SameSite`** — Controls whether the cookie is sent with cross-site requests. Your primary defense against CSRF.
- `Strict` — Cookie is never sent on cross-site requests. Safest option, but users clicking a link from an external site will appear logged out.
- `Lax` — Sent on top-level cross-site navigations (e.g. clicking a link), but not on subrequests from other sites. The modern browser default. Good balance for most apps.
- `None; Secure` — Sent on all cross-site requests. Required for third-party cookie scenarios. Must be paired with `Secure`.

**`Max-Age`** / **`Expires`** — Define when the cookie expires. Without these, it's a session cookie and disappears when the browser closes. With them, it persists across browser restarts.

**`Path`** — Restricts the cookie to a specific URL path prefix.

**`Domain`** — If set, the cookie is sent to all subdomains. If not set, it only goes to the exact origin that set it.

