## 8. Caching

Caching is one of the most impactful things you control from the backend. Getting it right means fewer requests hitting your servers and faster responses for users.

### Cache-Control

This is the primary header for controlling caching behavior. You set it on responses.

**`max-age=<seconds>`** — The response is considered fresh for this many seconds from when it was fetched. `Cache-Control: max-age=3600` means the browser can use the cached copy for one hour before checking again.

**`s-maxage=<seconds>`** — Like `max-age`, but specifically for shared caches (CDNs, reverse proxies). Overrides `max-age` for those intermediaries. This lets you give browsers a short TTL while letting your CDN hold the cache much longer.

**`public`** — The response can be stored by shared caches (CDNs). Use this for content that is the same for all users.

**`private`** — Only the end-user's browser may cache this. CDNs and shared proxies must not store it. Use for any user-specific data.

**`no-cache`** — Confusingly named. It doesn't mean "don't cache" — it means "cache it, but revalidate with the server before using it." The cache sends a conditional request; if the resource hasn't changed, the server returns `304` and the cached copy is used. Good for pages where freshness is important but you want to avoid re-downloading unchanged content.

**`no-store`** — Genuinely means "don't cache this at all, ever." Use for sensitive data like financial pages or anything with personal information.

**`must-revalidate`** — Once the response becomes stale, the cache must not serve it without revalidating first. Prevents stale content from being served when the network is temporarily unavailable.

**`immutable`** — Tells the browser this response will never change during its `max-age` window, so don't bother sending revalidation requests. Use this for content-hashed assets like `app.a3f4c5.js`. Eliminates unnecessary conditional requests on page reload.

**`stale-while-revalidate=<seconds>`** — Serve the stale cached copy immediately while fetching a fresh version in the background. Gives low latency while keeping content reasonably up to date.

### Caching Strategy by Resource Type

| Resource | Recommended Cache-Control |
|----------|--------------------------|
| HTML pages | `no-cache` — always revalidate |
| API responses (user-specific) | `private, no-store` |
| API responses (public, semi-stable) | `public, max-age=60, s-maxage=300` |
| Static assets (content-hashed) | `public, max-age=31536000, immutable` |
| Sensitive data | `no-store` |

