## 10. CORS

CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a browser security mechanism. By default, browsers block JavaScript from reading responses from a different origin. Your backend opts in to allow it by sending the right response headers. CORS is a **browser enforcement** — server-to-server requests are never affected by it.

**Same origin** means identical scheme + host + port. `https://api.example.com` and `https://app.example.com` are different origins even though they share a root domain.

### Simple Requests
GET/HEAD/POST requests with basic content types don't trigger a preflight. The browser sends the request directly, and your server must include `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` in the response to let the browser expose it to JavaScript.

### Preflight Requests
For custom headers, non-trivial content types, or methods other than GET/HEAD/POST, the browser first sends an `OPTIONS` request to check if the actual request is allowed. Your server must handle this preflight correctly or the actual request is never sent.

```
# Browser sends preflight
OPTIONS /api/data HTTP/1.1
Origin: https://app.example.com
Access-Control-Request-Method: POST
Access-Control-Request-Headers: Content-Type, Authorization

# Your server must respond
HTTP/1.1 204 No Content
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.example.com
Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, OPTIONS
Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type, Authorization
Access-Control-Max-Age: 86400
```

Set `Access-Control-Max-Age` to cache the preflight result — without it, the browser sends a preflight before every single request.

### Credentialed Requests
If your frontend sends cookies or an Authorization header cross-origin, you must explicitly allow credentials. You also cannot use `*` as the allowed origin — you must echo back the specific requesting origin.

```
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.example.com
Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true
```

### CORS Response Headers Summary
| Header | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` | Which origins are allowed (`*` or a specific origin) |
| `Access-Control-Allow-Methods` | Which request methods are allowed |
| `Access-Control-Allow-Headers` | Which request headers the client can send |
| `Access-Control-Expose-Headers` | Which response headers JavaScript is allowed to read |
| `Access-Control-Max-Age` | How long the browser can cache the preflight result |
| `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` | Whether cookies and auth headers are allowed cross-origin |

