## 11. Redirections

Redirects are responses where the server tells the client to go to a different URL via the `Location` header.

**`301 Moved Permanently`** — The resource has permanently moved. Browsers and search engines cache this and update links. The problem: many clients change the method to GET when following the redirect, even if the original was a POST.

**`302 Found`** — Temporary redirect with the same method-changing issue as 301. Often misused as a permanent redirect — prefer 301 when you mean permanent.

**`303 See Other`** — Always redirects to the target as a GET regardless of the original method. This is exactly what you want for the **Post/Redirect/Get (PRG) pattern**: after a POST that creates or submits something, redirect with 303 to a GET URL so the user doesn't accidentally resubmit on browser refresh.

**`307 Temporary Redirect`** — Temporary redirect that strictly preserves the original HTTP method. If the original was a POST, the redirected request is also a POST.

**`308 Permanent Redirect`** — Same as 307 but permanent. Use for permanent URL migrations where method preservation matters.

**Rule of thumb:** Use 303 for the PRG pattern. Use 307/308 when you need to preserve the method. Use 301 for permanent URL migrations of GET resources.

