## 14. Connection Management & HTTP Versions

Understanding how HTTP has evolved matters because it affects server configuration and performance characteristics.

**HTTP/1.1** made connections persistent by default — the same TCP connection handles multiple request-response pairs sequentially. This was a significant improvement over HTTP/1.0 where every request opened a new TCP connection. However, it has **head-of-line blocking**: if one request stalls, all queued requests wait behind it. Browsers work around this by opening up to 6 parallel connections per domain.

**HTTP/2** introduced **multiplexing** — multiple requests and responses can be in-flight simultaneously over a single connection using binary framing. This eliminates HTTP-level head-of-line blocking and makes opening multiple connections unnecessary. It also adds **HPACK header compression** (great for APIs with many repeated headers) and **server push** (proactively sending resources the client will need). To use HTTP/2, configure your server or reverse proxy with TLS — all browsers require HTTPS for HTTP/2.

**HTTP/3** runs over **QUIC** (UDP-based) instead of TCP. This solves TCP-level head-of-line blocking — a lost packet only stalls the one stream it belongs to, not the entire connection. It also has faster connection establishment and handles network switching (WiFi to mobile) more gracefully. Built-in TLS 1.3. Increasingly supported by CDNs and reverse proxies.

**Chunked Transfer Encoding** is how you stream a response when you don't know the total size upfront. Instead of buffering everything, you send `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` and write data in chunks. Useful for streaming large query results, real-time data feeds, and server-sent events.

