## 15. Proxy Servers & Reverse Proxies

Almost every production backend runs behind a **reverse proxy** (Nginx, Caddy, a cloud load balancer). It handles SSL termination, load balancing, caching, and rate limiting on your behalf.

Because SSL is terminated at the proxy, your app sees requests arrive over plain HTTP — the connection to your app is HTTP even though the client used HTTPS. The proxy adds headers so your app knows the original request details:

- **`X-Forwarded-For`** — The original client IP. Log this, not the direct connection IP (which would be the proxy's IP).
- **`X-Forwarded-Proto`** — Whether the original client connection was `http` or `https`. Critical for generating correct redirect URLs and enforcing HTTPS inside your app.
- **`X-Forwarded-Host`** — The original `Host` header from the client.
- **`Forwarded`** — The RFC 7239 standard header that combines all the above into one: `Forwarded: for=203.0.113.5;proto=https;host=example.com`.

**Security warning:** Always verify that these headers come from a trusted proxy IP. A client making a direct request to your app (bypassing the proxy) can set any `X-Forwarded-For` value they want — trusting it blindly lets them spoof their IP for rate limiting or logging purposes.

**CONNECT tunneling** is how proxies handle HTTPS traffic. When a browser wants to reach `https://example.com` through a proxy, it sends `CONNECT example.com:443 HTTP/1.1`. The proxy opens a TCP tunnel to that host, responds with `200 Connection Established`, then blindly forwards bytes in both directions. The TLS handshake happens through the tunnel, which is why a proxy can forward HTTPS traffic without being able to read it.

